1|What can each person do to protect the environment?\n2|Leave lights and air conditioners on standby even when leaving school\n3|Recycle, save water and electricity, and avoid littering outdoors\n4|Buy mostly disposable items to avoid washing dishes\n5|Assume recycling also fixes water pollution with no reduced use\n6|People can recycle, save water and electricity, avoid littering, and plant trees and plants.\n7|Every small action helps.\n8|Together, people can make a big difference.\n9|Why is it important to do experiments?\n10|Because an opinion is enough without testing a real case\n11|To learn about the world, test ideas, and understand how things work\n12|Only to pass time with no clear experiment question\n13|To watch once and rely on memory with no notes or repeats\n14|Experiments help us learn about the world, test ideas, understand how things work, and discover new things.\n15|Experiments are a scientific way to learn.\n16|They help students think like scientists.\n17|Before you test an idea, what is a hypothesis?\n18|A list of all the materials needed for the experiment.\n19|A prediction about what you think will happen and why.\n20|The final result recorded after the experiment is complete.\n21|The conclusion written after the data has been reviewed.\n22|A hypothesis is an educated prediction you can check with a fair test. You write it before you collect the results.\n23|A good hypothesis can be supported or not supported by evidence.\n24|Materials lists and final conclusions are not hypotheses.\n25|What does a plant need to grow and develop?\n26|Water and soil without sunlight\n27|Water, sunlight, and soil\n28|Light and soil without water from the roots\n29|Water and light without rich soil\n30|A plant needs water, sunlight, and soil to grow and stay healthy.\n31|Without water, a plant dries out.\n32|Without sunlight, a plant cannot photosynthesize.\n33|How is metal different from glass when it comes to conducting electricity?\n34|Metal conducts electricity, while glass does not.\n35|Both metal and glass conduct electricity equally well.\n36|Metal is always see-through like glass, but glass is always opaque.\n37|Glass conducts electricity better than any metal.\n38|Metal is a conductor, which means electricity can flow through it easily. Glass is an insulator, which means it does not let electricity pass through. That is why electrical wires are made of metal and wrapped in plastic or rubber for safety.\n39|Conductors allow electrons to move freely through the material, carrying an electric current.\n40|Insulators block the flow of electricity and are used to keep people safe around wires.\n41|What are the seasons of the year?\n42|Summer, winter, fall, and spring\n43|Only two seasons in a year\n44|The day-and-night cycle across the year\n45|Only mood cycles with no link to temperature or rain\n46|There are four seasons: summer (warm), winter (cold), fall, and spring.\n47|Seasons change because of Earth's tilt.\n48|Each season has different typical weather.\n49|Why are forests important for the environment?\n50|Forests produce oxygen, provide homes for animals, and help keep air and water clean.\n51|Forests are useful only as a source of building materials.\n52|Forests are important only for the animals living in them, not for people.\n53|Forests grow back immediately after they are cut down, so there is no concern.\n54|Forests produce oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide, and provide habitats for countless plants and animals. They also help regulate water cycles and protect the soil.\n55|Forests are sometimes called the 'lungs of the Earth' because they produce so much oxygen.\n56|Deforestation can harm both wildlife and the global climate.\n57|What is found on the upper part of a plant?\n58|Roots, usually\n59|Leaves and flowers\n60|Only a trunk with no leaves\n61|Prepared soil with no stem or leaf\n62|Leaves and flowers grow on the upper part of a plant, where they can take in sunlight.\n63|Leaves take in sunlight.\n64|Flowers are often colorful and attract insects.\n65|What is found on the lower part of a plant?\n66|Roots grow in the soil at the bottom of the plant.\n67|Roots anchor the plant.\n68|They take in water and minerals.\n69|What do plants need in order to grow?\n70|Sunlight, water, air, and materials from the soil\n71|Full darkness with no water\n72|Plants need sunlight to make food and grow. They also need water, air, and soil nutrients.\n73|Without light, a plant cannot grow well.\n74|What is found in a flower?\n75|Only leaves with no petals or nectar\n76|Petals and nectar that attract insects\n77|Only roots with no flower\n78|Barren soil with no stem or flower\n79|A flower has petals, pollen parts, and nectar that attract insects.\n80|Flowers are often colorful.\n81|Insects that visit flowers help with pollination.\n82|What is the first part of a plant to grow when a seed sprouts?\n83|The first part to grow is the flower.\n84|The first part to grow is a tiny root, which pushes down into the soil.\n85|The first part to grow is a large green leaf.\n86|The first part to grow is a flower full of new seeds.\n87|When a seed germinates, the very first part to grow is a small root called the radicle. It pushes down into the soil to anchor the plant and absorb water.\n88|The root is the first part to appear when a seed germinates.\n89|The seed uses stored energy until the plant grows leaves that can make food.\n90|Which material is transparent?\n91|Opaque stone\n92|Heavy metal\n93|Glass\n94|Solid wood\n95|Glass is transparent. You can see through it.\n96|Transparent materials let you see through them.\n97|That is useful for windows.\n98|Which material is shiny?\n99|Wood\n100|Soil\n101|Many metals are shiny because they reflect light.\n102|Metals can look shiny.\n103|Shine is one property people notice in metals.\n104|What happens to water when it gets very cold?\n105|It stays liquid\n106|It turns into ice\n107|It turns into a gas\n108|It disappears\n109|When water gets cold enough, it freezes into ice, a solid.\n110|This change is called freezing.\n111|It happens when the temperature drops below 32°F (0°C).\n112|Which of the following materials comes from trees?\n113|Metal.\n114|Plastic.\n115|Wood.\n116|Glass.\n117|Wood is a natural material that comes from trees. Metal comes from ores in the ground, plastic is made from oil, and glass is made from sand.\n118|Wood is a natural material that has been used for thousands of years.\n119|Trees are cut and processed to produce lumber for building and making objects.\n120|When does the Sun shine in the sky?\n121|The Sun shines at night when the Moon is not visible.\n122|The Sun shines during the daytime.\n123|The Sun shines only in the evening.\n124|The Sun shines only in the early morning.\n125|We see the Sun during the day because the part of Earth we are on is facing the Sun. When our side rotates away, it becomes night.\n126|The Sun appears to rise in the east and set in the west each day.\n127|We experience daytime when our side of Earth faces the Sun.\n128|When can you usually see the Moon in the sky?\n129|Only during the daytime, when the Sun is also out.\n130|Usually at night, when the sky is dark.\n131|Only very early in the morning before sunrise.\n132|Only during heavy thunderstorms.\n133|The Moon is most visible at night when the sky is dark. It reflects sunlight, which makes it glow in the night sky.\n134|The Moon reflects sunlight, which is why we can see it.\n135|Though the Moon can sometimes be seen during the day, it is easiest to spot at night.\n136|What is inside clouds?\n137|Completely dry air with no water\n138|Tiny water droplets\n139|Dust with no liquid water\n140|Strong wind with no moisture\n141|Clouds are made of tiny water droplets or ice crystals. When they get heavy, rain can fall.\n142|Clouds form when water vapor condenses.\n143|When droplets grow heavy, rain or snow can fall.\n144|What is the best way to protect our water sources?\n145|Use as much water as you want, since there is always enough.\n146|Avoid dumping waste into water and use water wisely.\n147|Water naturally cleans itself, so no action is needed.\n148|Fill water sources with as many natural materials as possible.\n149|Water is a limited resource. When waste, chemicals, or garbage enter rivers, lakes, and oceans, it harms the plants and animals that depend on clean water — including humans. Using water wisely and keeping it clean protects ecosystems and our drinking supply.\n150|Pollution can enter water from factories, farms, and littering.\n151|Clean water is essential for the survival of every living thing on Earth.\n152|Putting paper and used oil in the same trash bag with no sorting\n153|Using old materials again to make new things\n154|Leaving unsorted waste in the yard for a long time\n155|Burning mixed yard waste with uncontrolled smoke\n156|Recycling means using old materials again to make new products instead of throwing them away.\n157|What helps keep the air clean?\n158|Leaving a car engine running in a parking spot when you are not driving\n159|Using cars less and planting trees\n160|Burning yard waste with thick smoke near homes\n161|Assuming strong wind always removes every pollutant forever\n162|Using cars less and planting trees helps keep air cleaner.\n163|Clean air is important for health.\n164|Trees help clean the air.\n165|Which action best helps protect the environment?\n166|Burning garbage outdoors to reduce the amount of trash.\n167|Recycling, reducing waste, and avoiding water pollution.\n168|Leaving lights on all night to use less paper.\n169|Increasing factory output to boost production and jobs.\n170|Recycling turns used materials into new products instead of sending them to a landfill. Reducing waste means buying and using less in the first place. Keeping chemicals and trash out of water protects ecosystems. Together, these actions lower pollution and conserve natural resources.\n171|The three R's — Reduce, Reuse, Recycle — are a guide to lowering our impact on the environment.\n172|Small everyday choices, like bringing a reusable bag, add up to make a big difference.\n173|What is the purpose of a science experiment?\n174|To list all the steps of a procedure without actually testing anything.\n175|To ask a question and find out what happens by testing an idea.\n176|To memorize answers to science questions without any testing.\n177|To write predictions and never check whether they are correct.\n178|A science experiment helps you find answers by testing an idea. You set up a fair test, observe what happens, and record the results.\n179|Experiments start with a question and a prediction.\n180|The results of an experiment help answer the original question.\n181|Who should help with experiments?\n182|No one\n183|An adult who can help and make sure everything is safe\n184|Young children with no adult watching\n185|An adult who does not know the experiment or the risks\n186|An adult should help with experiments and check that everything is safe.\n187|Safety comes first.\n188|An adult can help keep the experiment safe.\n189|What happens when you do an experiment?\n190|Usually nothing changes if you do not write anything down\n191|You learn, see results, and understand how something works\n192|Free activity with no experiment question\n193|Quick actions with no order or comparison\n194|Doing an experiment helps you learn new things, see what happens, and understand how things work.\n195|Experiments help us learn.\n196|They are a great way to understand the world.\n197|Growth with no use of light or green pigment\n198|Making food from light, using water and carbon dioxide\n199|Only cellular respiration like in animals\n200|Drinking water with no converting light energy into food